# Summer Project Guidelines

1. Project Selection

Students should select a project based on their interests, technical background, and available time.

Before starting development, the student should clearly understand:

* What problem the project solves
* Who will use the system
* What the main features are
* What technologies may be required
* What challenges may be involved


2. Requirements

Students should identify both:
Functional Requirements

What the system should do.

Examples:
* User registration and login
* Creating and editing records
* Searching and filtering
* Notifications
* Booking
* Payments
* Reports
* AI-based recommendations

Non-Functional Requirements

How the system should perform.

Examples:
* Security
* Performance
* Usability
* Reliability
* Scalability
* Maintainability

3. System Design

Before implementation, students should prepare an appropriate system design.

Depending on the project, this may include:
* Use case diagrams
* Activity diagrams
* Class diagrams
* Sequence diagrams
* ER diagrams
* System architecture
* Database schema
* UI wireframes

4. Technology Selection

Students are free to select appropriate technologies.

The technology choice should be justified based on the project requirements rather than selected simply because it is popular.

Possible technologies may include:

* Web frameworks
* Mobile frameworks
* Relational databases
* NoSQL databases
* REST APIs
* Cloud services
* Machine learning libraries
* AI APIs
* Authentication services

 5. Testing

Students should test their applications systematically.

Testing may include:

* Unit testing
* Integration testing
* System testing
* Usability testing
* Security testing
* Performance testing

Students should document important test cases and their results.

 6. Documentation

The final project should be documented clearly.

Recommended documentation includes:

1. Introduction
2. Problem statement
3. Objectives
4. Requirements
5. System design
6. Implementation
7. Testing
8. Results
9. Limitations
10. Future work
11. Conclusion

 7. AI Projects

For AI-based projects, students should clearly identify:

* The AI problem being addressed
* Input data
* Data sources
* Data preprocessing
* Selected model or approach
* Evaluation metrics
* Results
* Limitations

Students should avoid using AI simply as a feature without understanding how and why the selected approach works.

8. Research-Oriented Extensions

Students who want to turn a project into a research project should identify a specific research question.

A research extension may involve:

* Comparing multiple approaches
* Developing a prediction model
* Improving an existing algorithm
* Creating a recommendation system
* Evaluating different architectures
* Studying user behavior
* Performing an experimental analysis



9. Originality

Students should develop their own implementation.

Existing applications, libraries, APIs, and open-source technologies may be used where appropriate, but their use should be properly acknowledged.

Students should clearly identify which parts of the project were developed by them and which components rely on external technologies or services.


10. Final Goal

The purpose of the summer project is not simply to produce a working application.

Students should demonstrate their ability to:

* Understand a real-world problem
* Analyze requirements
* Design a solution
* Implement the solution
* Test the system
* Evaluate the results
* Communicate their work
* Identify future improvements
